{
  "teamId": 113,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Cincinnati Reds opened the year with genuine promise, surging to a 17-9 April record that had the Queen City buzzing. Nathaniel Lowe's walk-off blast and Eugenio Suárez's clutch three-run shot in the opening series set an electric tone, and the offense — anchored by young slugger Sal Stewart and veteran pop from Suárez — kept the Reds punching above their weight through the first month. It felt, briefly, like the rebuild had arrived ahead of schedule.\n\nMay and June told a darker story. The rotation cracked under the pressure, with Graham Ashcraft and Tony Santillan surrendering pivotal home runs in high-leverage spots, and the bullpen — particularly Emilio Pagán — became a recurring source of heartbreak. The Reds went a combined 19-34 across those two months, turning a promising start into a .500 mirage. Errors, double plays, and late-inning surrenders became the defining images of a team that simply could not hold leads when it mattered most.\n\nJuly offered a brief reprieve. The Reds played competitive baseball at 13-12, fueled by a red-hot Sal Stewart — who slugged his 21st homer in a critical late-July moment — and a continued spark from Suárez in the middle of the order. Stewart's clutch single on July 31st capped a stretch that reminded Cincinnati fans what this lineup could do when it clicked. The young core showed flashes of the franchise's future even as the postseason window narrowed.\n\nAugust and September could not sustain it. The pitching staff continued to leak runs — 826 allowed on the season — and crucial defensive miscues, including a brutal Dane Myers error in late August, unraveled promising moments. Matt McLain's grand slam on August 20th was a highlight, but Brock Burke's error in the same game epitomized the Reds' inability to finish. At 75-87, Cincinnati's 2026 season is a story of a young team still learning how to win, with Stewart's emergence and Suárez's veteran leadership as the most compelling reasons for hope heading into the offseason.",
